<.start_page current="api">
  <section
    id="api-lede"
    class="mb-10"
  >
    <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      The Bitblocks API
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      JSON over HTTP, from <code class="font-mono text-base">https://bitblocks.app/api/v1</code>.
      This page covers how to call it and the endpoints to start with.
      The linked examples open in a signed-in browser; signed out, they answer 401.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    id="access"
    aria-labelledby="access-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="access-heading">
      Who can call what
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table
        id="access-table"
        class="w-full text-left text-sm"
      >
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-600 dark:border-gray-700 dark:text-gray-400">
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium"
            >
              Caller
            </th>
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium"
            >
              Reaches
            </th>
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium"
            >
              Requests a minute
            </th>
            <th
              scope="col"
              class="py-2 font-medium"
            >
              State
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700 dark:divide-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              Anyone
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">/status</code>,
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">/price/bsv</code>,
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">/support</code>
              and the Bitcom registry exports
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono tabular-nums">
              {@rate_limits.anonymous}, per IP
            </td>
            <td class="py-2">
              <.status kind="today" />
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              <.text_link href="/sign-in">Signed in with GitHub</.text_link>
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              The whole API, from the browser you signed in with
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono tabular-nums">
              {@rate_limits.signed_in}, per user
            </td>
            <td class="py-2">
              <.status kind="signed_in" />
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              A key
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              The whole API, from your own server
            </td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">
              The key's own budget
            </td>
            <td class="py-2">
              <.status kind="coming" />
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        Sign in, and you can <.text_link href="/start#step-get-a-key">get a trial key</.text_link> for yourself.
        A key goes in the header, as <code class="font-mono text-xs">Authorization: Bearer bbk_...</code>
        or <code class="font-mono text-xs">x-api-key: bbk_...</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <.text_link href="/pricing">Pricing</.text_link> covers what comes after the free tier.
      </p>
      <p>
        Block and transaction pages are open to anyone, so a link you share simply works.
        The JSON behind them is the API, and needs a session or a key.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="responses"
    aria-labelledby="responses-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="responses-heading">
      Reading a response
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        Most answers carry the result in <code class="font-mono text-xs">data</code>.
        A list adds <code class="font-mono text-xs">meta</code>, and you page by passing
        <code class="font-mono text-xs">next_cursor</code>
        back as <code class="font-mono text-xs">?cursor=</code>.
      </p>
      <.code_panel
        label="A page of transactions, abridged"
        code={envelope_example()}
      />
      <p>
        <code class="font-mono text-xs">meta.complete</code>
        is false while any block in a listing's range is still syncing, so an empty page isn't always the end of the story.
      </p>
      <.code_panel
        label="Without a session or key: 401"
        code={unauthorized_example()}
      />
      <p>
        Too many requests in a minute gets a 429 with a <code class="font-mono text-xs">Retry-After</code> header, and an unknown or revoked key gets a 401.
        A transaction we don't hold gets a 404, which can simply mean the sync hasn't reached its block yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="partial-answers"
    aria-labelledby="partial-answers-heading"
    class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-4 dark:border-gray-700"
  >
    <h2
      id="partial-answers-heading"
      class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white"
    >
      Answers that say how sure they are
    </h2>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        An output is known to be unspent only once the index reaches the tip, so address and script answers carry a status: <code class="font-mono text-xs">exact</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-xs">partial</code>
        or <code class="font-mono text-xs">unknown</code>.
        While the sync climbs, expect the last two, and treat unknown as unknown.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you need a whole-chain balance today, <.text_link href="https://docs.whatsonchain.com/">WhatsOnChain's API</.text_link> is the place to look.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    :for={group <- @groups}
    id={group.id}
    aria-labelledby={"#{group.id}-heading"}
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id={"#{group.id}-heading"}>
      {group.title}
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      {group.lede}
    </p>
    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 border-y border-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700 dark:border-gray-700">
      <div
        :for={row <- group.rows}
        class="py-3"
      >
        <dt class="font-mono text-xs break-all text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          {row.method} {row.path}
          <span
            :if={row.access == :open}
            class="ml-2 font-sans font-semibold text-green-700 dark:text-green-400"
          >
            open
          </span>
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {row.does}
          <a
            :if={row.example}
            href={row.example}
            class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs break-all text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300"
          >
            {row.example}
          </a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section
    id="more"
    aria-labelledby="more-heading"
    class="mb-8"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="more-heading">
      Going further
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      This list is the core of the API, and
      <.text_link href="https://github.com/afomi/bitblocks">the code is open</.text_link>
      if you want to see all of it.
      Proofs are explained in full in
      <.text_link href="https://github.com/afomi/bitblocks/blob/develop/docs/MERKLE_PROOFS.md">the merkle proofs doc</.text_link>.
    </p>
  </section>
</.start_page>
